 
3713 Problem:-
You are given a string s consisting of lowercase English letters.
A substring of s is called balanced if all distinct characters in the substring appear the same number of times.
Return the length of the longest balanced substring of s.

Example 1:
Input: s = "abbac"
Output: 4
Explanation:
The longest balanced substring is "abba" because both distinct characters 'a' and 'b' each appear exactly 2 times.

Example 2:
Input: s = "zzabccy"
Output: 4
Explanation:
The longest balanced substring is "zabc" because the distinct characters 'z', 'a', 'b', and 'c' each appear exactly 1 time.
​​​​​​​
Example 3:
Input: s = "aba"
Output: 2
Explanation:
​​​​​​​One of the longest balanced substrings is "ab" because both distinct characters 'a' and 'b' each appear exactly 1 time. Another longest balanced substring is "ba".

Constraints:-
1 <= s.length <= 1000
s consists of lowercase English letters.

===============================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-
Intuition:-
Balanced substring is a substring where all unique characters have the same frequency.
So we can keep track of number of unique characters uniq in a window and maximum frequency maxfreq and then if string is balanced every character has a frequency = maxfreq and len of window is cur = uniq * maxfreq.
This is the criteria of balanced substring.
Using this criteria we can iterate over all possible substrings s[l..r], calculate uniq and maxfreq and find maximum across all balanced substrings.

Approach:-
The question is how to calculate uniq and maxfreq efficiently.
First we will iterate over left index of the substring l
for l in range(n):
Then we will iterate over right index of the substring r and keep track of counts of every char, number of unique chars and max frequency
cnt = [0] * 26  # Counts of every char
uniq = maxfreq = 0  # Number of uniq chars and max frequency
for r in range(l, n)
Then to efficiently update uniq and maxfreq we will use the fact that our substring is only increasing. So if for current char i cnt[i] == 0, then it's a new unique char and we update uniq
uniq += cnt[i] == 0
Then we update cnt[i] and check if a new value is bigger then maxfreq, then update maxfreq
cnt[i] += 1
if cnt[i] > maxfreq:  # Update max frequency
    maxfreq = cnt[i]
Now we have uniq and maxfreq updated we check our balanced substring criteria and try to update the result
cur = r - l + 1
if uniq * maxfreq == cur and cur > result:result = cur

Complexity:-
C — size of the alphabet = 26 by description
Time complexity: O(n 2+nC) = O(n 2)
Space complexity: O(C) = O(1)

Code:-
class Solution {
    public int longestBalanced(String s) {
        int n = s.length();

        // Transform char -> int
        int[] a = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) 
            a[i] = s.charAt(i) - 'a';

        int result = 0;
        for (int l = 0; l < n; l++) {
            // Early exit, can't be bigger
            if (n - l <= result) 
                break;

            int[] cnt = new int[26]; // Counts of every char
            int uniq = 0, maxfreq = 0; // Number of uniq chars and maximum frequency
            for (int r = l; r < n; r++) {
                int i = a[r];

                // There was no this char before => one more uniq
                if (cnt[i] == 0) 
                    uniq++;

                cnt[i]++;
                // Update max frequency
                if (cnt[i] > maxfreq) 
                    maxfreq = cnt[i];

                // Check if all uniq chars have maxfreq frequency then update the result
                int cur = r - l + 1;
                if (uniq * maxfreq == cur && cur > result)
                    result = cur;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}
